  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nA mid/upper trough was situated over the Oklahoma Panhandle area \nearly this afternoon and it is progged to shear eastward while \nfilling as we move into the evening hours. Meanwhile, a cold front \nbisecting central KS will gradually move south and east across \nthe area this evening and tonight. Widespread showers and \nthunderstorms are expected to develop ahead of the mid/upper \ntrough targeting south central KS this afternoon and evening. We\ncontinue to see a very moist airmass with PWATs at or above \n1.75 inches which is roughly 200% of normal for early Oct. \nREFS/HREF neighborhood probabilities of exceeding 2 inches of \nrain in 6 hours have increased considerably (>80%) for those \nalong and south of Hwy 56, across much of south central KS and \nthe flood watch was expanded for much of the area along and west\nof the Flint Hills through midnight. Some breaks in the clouds \non the eastern periphery of weak sfc low could allow for some \nlocal heating leading to a strong storm or two near the OK state\nline. Considerable cloud cover remains, so confidence remains \nfairly low but a locally strong storm or two could be possible. \nA stable post-frontal airmass will gradually build southward \nacross the region tonight with rain chances diminishing as we \nmove through the overnight hours.\n\nFriday--Thursday...a stable post-frontal regime will be in place Fri-\nSat with breezy winds gradually subsiding as we move through the day \non Friday. The mid/upper flow will transition to a NW flow regime as \nwe move through the week with mild and dry weather anticipated.\nAn extended period of dry and warm conditions are anticipated \nwith above normal temperatures returning to the area Tuesday- \nThursday.",
